## [upstream] complex: arccosh returns +0 on the positive real axis

Commit: d6ec47d87

`gsl_complex_arccosh()` was implemented as `arccos` followed by a
multiplication by `i`, with the sign chosen from the imaginary part of the
intermediate result.  For a real argument the intermediate is real, so
`arccos()` returns it with a zero imaginary part and multiplying by `i`
negates the real part: the result carried a **negative** zero real part,
where `acosh()` and `gsl_complex_arccosh_real()` return a positive zero.

The value was otherwise correct - only the sign of zero differed - but
`gsl_complex_arccosh_real()` exists for exactly this case, and
`gsl_complex_arccos()` already special-cases a real argument for the same
reason, so the fix delegates `GSL_IMAG(a) == 0` to
`gsl_complex_arccosh_real()`.

The existing tables in `complex/results.h` already expected `+0` for
`arccosh(1+0i)`, but the harness compares magnitudes and cannot see the
sign.  The new vectors compare against `gsl_complex_arccosh_real()` and
assert the sign of the real part.  With the change reverted the test
reports three failures (-1, 0.5 and 1, the points whose result is real and
zero); complex arguments are untouched.

Files: `complex/math.c`, `complex/test_source.c`.

See Savannah bug #47345.


## [upstream] interpolation: NaN for a point out of the 2D interpolation domain

Commit: 2b4e2f1e5

`interp2d_eval()`, the wrapper shared by every `gsl_interp2d_eval*_e()`
function, rejected an out-of-range point with `GSL_ERROR(...)` and nothing
else.  `GSL_ERROR` calls the error handler and returns the code; it does
not touch the output argument.  So on a domain error
`gsl_interp2d_eval_e()` left the caller's `z` at whatever it held, and the
non-`_e` convenience wrappers, which declare a local `double z` and return
it regardless of the status, returned an uninitialised value.

Both boundary checks now store `GSL_NAN` in the result before returning
`GSL_EDOM`.

The regression test calls the evaluator with `x` and with `y` below the
domain, seeding the output with a sentinel so that the old behaviour fails
deterministically, and checks the non-`_e` wrapper as well.  The default
error handler aborts the process on `GSL_ERROR`, so the test disables it
around the calls and restores it afterwards.  With the change reverted two
of the three assertions fail.

Files: `interpolation/interp2d.c`, `interpolation/test2d.c`.

See Savannah bug #60371.


## [upstream] randist: parse the gsl-randist seed as unsigned long

Commit: aebe57a5c

`gsl-randist` read its seed with `atol()`, which returns `long` and
saturates at `LONG_MAX`, while `gsl_rng_default_seed` is `unsigned long`.
Any seed above `LONG_MAX` was therefore silently truncated to `LONG_MAX`
on LP64 platforms, where `unsigned long` can represent it.  On this build
(LLP64) a seed of `3000000000` came out as `2147483647`.

The seed is now read with `strtoul()` at base 0, so decimal, octal and
hexadecimal seeds are accepted exactly as before.  The accompanying
diagnostic printed the value with `%ld` for what is an `unsigned long`;
that is corrected to `%lu`.

Not changed: `n` is still read with `atol()` into an `unsigned int`; that
is a separate argument and out of scope for this report.

File: `gsl-randist.c`.

See Savannah bug #54077.


## [upstream] linalg: define the LU pivot array before building the permutation

Commit: d73ba5300

`gsl_linalg_LU_decomp()` and `gsl_linalg_complex_LU_decomp()` allocate
`ipiv` and call `LU_decomp_L3()`, which for `N > 24` recurses on
sub-blocks.  When a singular pivot is met in the leading sub-block the
recursion returns immediately without writing the tail of `ipiv`, and the
caller then reads the whole array to build the permutation.  That produced
a permutation built from uninitialised memory, and `p->data[pivi]` could
index outside the permutation.  `LU_decomp_L2()`, used for `N <= 24`,
already wrote every entry even when singular, so the defect only showed on
larger matrices.

`ipiv` is now initialized to the identity immediately after allocation, in
both functions.  `LU_decomp_L3()` recurses on views of that same buffer, so
every entry is defined before any read; entries the factorization does not
reach stay no-ops, which is the correct "no interchange" placeholder.  This
is preferable to the submitted `if (!status)` guard, which also discarded
the pivots the Level 2 path had already computed.

The test uses a 128x128 diagonal matrix with a zero leading pivot.  No row
interchange is required, so the permutation must be the identity, and the
rank-deficient leading block drives `LU_decomp_L3()` down the early-return
path.  With the change reverted 112 of the 128 permutation entries are
wrong - the array is filled from recycled heap memory, which in this build
appeared shifted by one.  A hard guarantee against the uninitialised read
would need valgrind or MSan; the test is written so that ordinary heap
contents are enough to catch it.

Files: `linalg/lu.c`, `linalg/luc.c`, `linalg/test_lu.c`,
`linalg/test_luc.c`.

See Savannah bug #66026.


