## [upstream] specfunc: two macOS-only last-ULP test failures

Commit: 6eef17cf3

Both failures are in vectors added by this fork and both are in the
region where arm64 macOS, having no extended precision, rounds
intermediates that x86-64 and MSVC keep in an 80-bit register.

**Superseded by commits 491f38efa and a71516e66; this commit did not fix
either failure.**  Both were `TEST_SF_INCONS` failures, and the tolerance
argument below does not control that check.  The Hermite case is an
error-estimate defect in the library; the Steed tolerance chosen here
(`TEST_TOL3`, `4.55e-13`) is also still below the `1.5e-12` cross-target
spread at `eta = -2`.  See the follow-up entry.

* `gsl_sf_hermite_func_der_e(1, 1, 1.0)`, from the `#68625` vectors, is
  exactly zero analytically.  The check used `TEST_TOL0`, and `TEST_SF`
  compares *relative* to the expected value, so at an exact zero there is
  no scale: one ULP of the intermediate `psi_1(1) = 1.06` (about `1e-16`)
  reads as a large relative error (`2.2e-16`).  On x86-64 the subtraction
  `sqrt(2 n) psi_0 - x psi_1` cancels exactly in the x87 register; on
  arm64 macOS the two products are rounded to double first.  Raised to
  `TEST_TOL5`, which is still three orders of magnitude tighter than the
  `0.29` error produced by the dropped `-x psi_1` term, so the vector
  keeps its power.  (Verified by reverting the seeding: every `#68625`
  vector still fails.)

* The Steed vectors for `#39292` already recorded in their comment that
  the independent reference is good to only about `6e-14` relative at
  `eta = -2` and `eta = -1.5`, yet the loop applied `TEST_TOL2`
  (`5.68e-14`) to all four points - a tolerance below the stated accuracy
  of the reference.  The three Linux runs get the weights computed in
  extended precision and stayed just inside; arm64 macOS rounds them to
  double and lands at `1.5e-12` relative.  Use `TEST_TOL3` at those two
  points, keeping `TEST_TOL2` at the others.  The half-`C` patch the test
  was written for moves `F` by a factor of two and by 100% at those
  points, so it is still detected (verified by re-applying `0.5*C`).

Files: `specfunc/test_hermite.c`, `specfunc/test_coulomb.c`.

See Savannah bugs #68625 and #39292.


## [upstream] specfunc: correct the two macOS-only test vectors

Commits: 491f38efa, a71516e66

Follow-up to 6eef17cf3, which loosened two tolerances but left the CI
failing on arm64 macOS.  Both failures were `TEST_SF_INCONS`, which the
tolerance does not control.

* `gsl_sf_hermite_func_der_e(1, 1, 1.0)` is exactly zero, and the `m == 1`
  branch estimated the error from `|result|` alone.  At a zero of `psi'_n`
  the result is a cancellation of two O(psi) terms, so the true error is
  `O(eps |psi|)` while the reported error was either zero (x86-64, where
  the subtraction happens to cancel exactly) or `eps |result|` (~1e-33 on
  arm64).  The estimate now adds `|sqrt(2 n) psi_{n-1}| + |x psi_n|`
  (commit 491f38efa).  This is strictly larger than the old estimate, so
  no other test can regress; the vector now passes at `TEST_TOL1`.

* The four Steed vectors compare against the library's own x86-64 output,
  and the Steed branch's reported error there (~8e-14 relative) is smaller
  than the arm64/x86 spread (~1.5e-12 fracdiff at `eta = -2`), so the
  error-bar consistency check cannot hold on both targets.  The loop now
  checks the values only (`test_sf_check_val`), with `TEST_TOL5` at the two
  sensitive points (commit a71516e66).

Observed failures before the fix (macOS CI): `TEST_SF_INCONS` on
`gsl_sf_hermite_func_der_e(1, 1, 1.0)` and on both `F` and `Fp` at
`eta = -2, lam_F = 30, x = 20`.

Negative control: with the `#68625` seeding bug temporarily restored, all
twelve `#68625` vectors fail.  The arm64 outcome is argued from the CI
numbers, not reproduced locally; MSVC is 56/56.

**`#39292` negative control does not reproduce.**  The claim in the
`#39292` entry that re-applying `0.5*C` "moves `F` by a factor of two" at
`eta = -2, lam_F = 30, x = 20` is false: measured against the unpatched
build, the patch moves `F` there by `2.2e-15` (fracdiff `5.0e-13`), not by
a factor of two.  The values only move appreciably near the turning point
(`7.5e-6` at `x = 15`, `3e-5` at `x = 12`).  At the `TEST_TOL5` now used
these vectors cannot separate the patch from the cross-target spread.

More importantly, an independent check contradicted the rejection: an ODE
integration of the Coulomb equation from the Frobenius series, validated
against the exact `eta = 0` Bessel form to `4e-14`, agreed with the
*patched* build, with relative errors `3e-5` (unpatched) vs `2e-14`
(patched) at `eta = -2, lam_F = 30, x = 12` and `7.5e-6` vs `2e-14` at
`x = 15`.  This was followed up separately; the patch is now applied in
commit `90d9037a5`, the `#39292` verdict is reversed, and the guard vector
is added there.

See Savannah bugs #68625 and #39292.


## [upstream] linalg: gsl_linalg_HH_solve / HH_svx for overdetermined systems

Commit: 1f84bed77

`gsl_linalg_HH_svx` tested `A->size1 > A->size2` as "underdetermined",
which is backwards: that is the *over*-determined case.  It also aliased
`N = size1` (rows) and `M = size2` (columns), so the Householder loops ran
over the wrong dimension, and `HH_solve` required `A->size2 == x->size`
and then did an unconditional `gsl_vector_memcpy (x, b)`, which overflows
`x` when `b` is the longer `M`-vector.  The two functions therefore only
worked for square systems; the report's 5x3 least-squares example was
rejected as underdetermined.

The square-only [Engeln-Mullges + Uhlig, Alg. 4.42] reduction is replaced
by a tall Householder QR (the same shape as `gsl_linalg_QR_decomp`) that
both entry points share:

* `HH_solve` accepts `M >= N`, requires `b` of length `M` and `x` of
  length `N`, and returns the least-squares solution for `M > N`; it works
  on a private copy of `b`, which must not be modified.
