## [upstream] complex: preserve special values in GSL_SET_COMPLEX

Commit: 631da98f8

`GSL_SET_COMPLEX` has two definitions.  The fallback used when
`<complex.h>` is absent stores the components directly, but the native
C11 branch builds the value as

    (*(zp) = (x) + I * (y))

which is a complex multiplication and addition, not a store.  Those
operations round, so `GSL_SET_COMPLEX(&z, -0.0, 0.0)` gave `+0.0` for
the real part, and `GSL_SET_COMPLEX(&z, 0.0, INFINITY)` gave `NaN` - the
`I * y` product computes `0 * inf` - instead of `+0.0`.

The native definition now assigns the components directly through the
same `GSL_REAL`/`GSL_IMAG` lvalues that `GSL_SET_REAL`/`GSL_SET_IMAG`
already use, so signed zeros and non-finite parts survive.  This needs
no `CMPLX`, which matters because neither posted patch is usable:
`CMPLX.diff` uses the `double` variant for all three complex types (so
`complex float` and `complex long double` targets lose precision), and
the `_Generic` patch dispatches on the pointer type and calls
`CMPLXF`/`CMPLXL`, neither of which MinGW's `<complex.h>` defines at all
(verified with `gcc -E -dM`).

A block of exact checks was added to `complex/test_source.c`, next to the
`#47345` signed-zero check, and runs under both `test` (legacy) and
`test_c11` (native).  It covers the reported `-0.0`, a non-finite part,
signed zeros for `float` and `long double`, and a `1.0L + LDBL_EPSILON`
round trip that would catch a `CMPLX(double)`-style truncating fix.

Negative control: with the old macro the native branch fails four of the
added checks (`-0.0` real, `(0, +inf)` real, `float -0.0`,
`long double -0.0`); the legacy build passes throughout.  The full MSVC
suite is 56/56, and a MinGW C11 build of `complex.test_c11` passes all
10201 checks (and fails the same four with the macro reverted).

Files: `complex/gsl_complex.h`, `complex/test.c`,
`complex/test_source.c`, `doc/complex.rst`, `doc_texinfo/complex.texi`.

See Savannah bug #65912.


