## [upstream] movstat: align the embedded accumulator state

Commit: 4e4a88242

The moving-window accumulators keep their state in a single block that
the caller provides, and derive the address of each object in it by
adding the previous object's byte size.  Two of those objects - the
min/max accumulator's deques and every accumulator's ring buffer - hold
a pointer, so they require pointer alignment (8 bytes on the 64-bit
targets).  The offsets are not always multiples of that:

  * `mmacc`: `state->maxque = state->minque + deque_size(n + 1)` and
    `deque_size(n+1) = sizeof(deque) + 4*(n+1)`, which is 4 modulo 8
    when the window size n is even;
  * `qnacc`: `state->rbuf` follows a `5*n` int array, so its offset is
    `sizeof(state) + 52*n`, which is 4 modulo 8 when n is odd.

This is undefined behaviour.  Clang's alignment sanitizer reports it as

    movstat/deque.c:58: runtime error: member access within misaligned
    address 0x... for type 'deque', which requires 8 byte alignment

and the equivalent `ringbuf.c:61` diagnostic for the Q_n case.  This is
Savannah bug #59834; the bug report covers the deque case (hit through
`gsl_filter_rmedian`, which uses the min/max accumulator).  The Q_n case
was found by auditing every accumulator for the same pattern and is not
in the report.  Sn, qqr, MAD, mean/variance, sum, median and the user
function accumulators were checked and are already aligned.

A portable alignment helper is added to `movstat/ringbuf.c`, which every
accumulator includes.  C89 has no `alignof`, but a struct with a leading
char places its next member at an offset equal to that member's
alignment; the union of `ringbuf` and `double` gives the largest
alignment any of these objects needs.  `ringbuf_align()` rounds a byte
offset up to that boundary, and `mmacc_size`/`mmacc_init` and
`qnacc_size`/`qnacc_init` now use it for every object offset.  No
computed value changes.

Negative control: no ordinary `ctest` can catch this, because the defect
is undefined behaviour with no numeric symptom on x86-64 (the results
are the same before and after).  The negative control is therefore a
sanitizer run: a driver that exercises `gsl_movstat_accum_minmax` and
`gsl_movstat_accum_Qn`, compiled with

    clang -fsanitize=alignment -I . -I build-cmake -I movstat \
        movstat/mmacc.c drv.c -o drv.exe

reports the misaligned accesses at `deque.c:58-61` and `ringbuf.c:61`
before the change and is silent after it (LLVM clang 18.1.1, inside the
VS 2022 BuildTools environment; MSVC has no UBSan and MinGW has no
`libubsan`).  The full MSVC `ctest` suite is 56/56 both before and
after.

Files: `movstat/ringbuf.c`, `movstat/mmacc.c`, `movstat/qnacc.c`.

See Savannah bug #59834.



## [upstream] randist: regression test for gsl_ran_beta with small parameters

Commit: d64cc4d93

The code fix for this defect is already upstream - commit
`05c5b5179` ("bug fix for #47646 (gsl_ran_beta)", 2016-09-15) added the
`(a <= 1 && b <= 1)` branch to `randist/beta.c`, and this fork's copy is
identical to `savannah/master`.  Savannah bug #47646, however, has stayed
open because the change came without a test: the maintainer's last comment
says the PDF integration test cannot be used, as the beta density diverges
at 0 and 1 for these parameters.

The defect: below `a = 1` a gamma variate is exactly zero a large fraction
of the time (`gsl_ran_gamma(1e-5, 1)` returns exact zero for 198470 of
200000 draws here), so the old `x1 / (x1 + x2)` computed `0/0` and returned
NaN for roughly 98 % of the samples.  The upstream fix samples
`Beta(a, a)` by rejection instead.

The test adds `test_beta_small` to `randist/test.c` and checks it with the
existing `testMoments` harness: with `a = b = 1e-5` the distribution is
symmetric about `1/2`, so `P(X < 1/2) = P(X > 1/2) = 1/2`.  The intervals
are `(-0.5, 0.5)` and `(0.5, 1.5)`, not `(0, 1/2)` and `(1/2, 1)`: half of
the mass sits exactly on the atoms 0 and 1, and the strict inequalities in
`testMoments` would discard those samples.  The two vectors are registered
at the end of `main`, after the last test that reads the shared RNG stream,
so no existing statistical test shifts phase.

The `a = b` symmetry is the reference; it does not use GSL's own CDF.

Negative control: with the `(a <= 1 && b <= 1)` branch reverted to the
plain gamma ratio the two vectors fail - `0.00759` and `0.00783` observed
against `0.5` expected, 2 failures.  With the fix in place `randist_test`
reports 152/152 and the full `ctest` suite 56/56.

File: `randist/test.c`.  See Savannah bug #47646.


## [rejected] roots: brent "produces wrong results under valgrind"

Not applied - not reproducible.

Savannah bug #58763 reports that the one dimensional Brent solver,
`gsl_root_fsolver_brent`, exits on its first iteration with `root = 5` and
the bracket collapsed to `[5, 5]` when the manual's example (`x^2 - 5` on
`[0, 5]`) is run under Valgrind, while `gsl_root_fsolver_bisection` and
`gsl_root_fsolver_falsepos` are unaffected.

The reported output follows from exactly one condition: the third point of
the Brent state, `c` and `fc`, being zero at the first iterate.  With
`c = fc = 0` the same-sign fix-up at the top of `brent_iterate` is skipped,
the `|fc| < |fb|` swap then sets `b = c = 5`, so `m = 0` and `fb = 0`, and
the routine returns `root = 5` with `x_lower = x_upper = 5`.  Brent is the
only bracketing solver whose state carries a third point -
`bisection_state_t` and `falsepos_state_t` hold only `f_lower` and
`f_upper` - which is why only Brent was affected.

`brent_init` already writes all eight state fields, including
`state->c = x_upper` and `state->fc = f_upper`, so that precondition
cannot occur.  The file is identical to the GSL 2.5 and 2.6 releases and
has not changed functionally since 1999, and a 2025 comment on the tracker
also failed to reproduce the report with 2.6.  The reporter's binary must
therefore have used a library whose `brent_init` did not initialise
`c`/`fc`; the source it names does.

Verified on the fork: the reporter's own `demo.c`/`demo_fn.c`, linked
against the current shared library, prints the report's expected
(non-Valgrind) table - first iteration `[1.0000000, 5.0000000]`
`root 1.0000000`, convergence on iteration 6 to `2.2360634`.  `ctest -R
roots` passes.  No source change.

See Savannah bug #58763.


## [upstream] sys, specfunc: avoid signed overflow in pow_int for n = INT_MIN

Commit: 41b1e2c00

`gsl_pow_int()` negated its exponent in signed arithmetic when the
exponent was negative:

    if(n < 0) {
      x = 1.0/x;
      un = -n;
    }

For `n = INT_MIN`, `-n` overflows `int`: undefined behaviour, which a
compiler is free to exploit.  Commit `9493ac014` (2010) converted the
loop counter to `unsigned int` to stop the infinite loop that the
overflow caused, but left the negation itself undefined.  Under clang
`-O2` with LTO the undefined result is used and
`gsl_pow_int(1.0000001, INT_MIN)` returns `1` instead of `5.4447103e-94`,
failing the sys vector added by that same commit.  On MSVC the negation
wraps to `2^31` and the vector passes, which is why the report is a
clang/Linux one.

`specfunc/pow_int.c` has the same negation, and `9493ac014` did not touch
it at all:

    if(n < 0) {
      n = -n;
      ...

There the undefined negation leaves `n = INT_MIN`, and the arithmetic
right shift in the loop keeps `n` negative, so `gsl_sf_pow_int_e()` loops
forever for `n = INT_MIN`.

Both functions now convert to `unsigned int` before negating,
`un = -(unsigned int)n`, which is well defined and leaves the true
magnitude `2^31`.  The specfunc loop counter is `unsigned int` as well.

Tests: the sys vector `gsl_pow_int(1.0000001, INT_MIN)` (from
`9493ac014`) already exercises the reported case.  `specfunc/test_sf.c`
gains two vectors for `gsl_sf_pow_int_e()`: the reported magnitude,
compared with a relaxed `1e-6` tolerance because repeated squaring
accumulates rounding that the function's small reported error does not
cover, and `(-1)^INT_MIN = 1` for the parity.  The direct vector is the
one the report quotes; `pow()` was checked as the independent reference.

Negative control: with the negation reverted, a driver containing the
same two `gsl_pow_int` bodies compiled with gcc `-O2` returns `1` - the
reporter's clang `-O2 -flto` result - and reverting the specfunc negation
makes `test_pow_int` loop forever.  Neither reproduces on MSVC, where the
overflow silently wraps.  Full `ctest` suite 56/56 on MSVC after the
change.

Files: `sys/pow_int.c`, `specfunc/pow_int.c`, `specfunc/test_sf.c`.

See Savannah bug #68495.


## [upstream] specfunc: polygamma at negative arguments

Commit: e9f69933a

`gsl_sf_psi_n_e()` returned `GSL_EDOM` for every `n >= 2` with `x <= 0`
that was not a pole.  The function is finite and smooth at those
arguments, and the `n = 0` and `n = 1` cases (`gsl_sf_psi_e()`,
`gsl_sf_psi_1_e()`) already accepted them, so the restriction was both
wrong and internally inconsistent.  Reported by Paul Zimmermann; the bug
carries a whole-file drop-in replacement (`psi_dropin.c`) rather than a
diff.  See Savannah bug #67728.

Negative `x` is now shifted onto the positive axis with the recurrence
`psi^(n)(x+1) = psi^(n)(x) + (-1)^n n! / x^(n+1)`, applied
`m = ceil(-x) = -floor(x)` times:

    psi^(n)(x) = psi^(n)(x+m) - (-1)^n n! sum_{k=0}^{m-1} 1/(x+k)^(n+1).

Because `0 < x+m <= 1` and every `x+k` is negative, the correction term
is positive and is added directly.  The reporter's code used `size_t`
and C99 mid-block declarations, formed `n!` directly (which overflows
beyond `n = 170`), and did not account for cancellation; here the terms
go through `gsl_sf_lnfact_e()` and a Kahan-compensated sum, and the
error estimate carries `sum |term|` so the cancellation is reported
rather than hidden.

The reporter flagged two limitations.  Neither is as stated:

* "sign is incorrect for n >= 5".  The `(-1)^n` sign is correct.  For
  `n = 5, x = -1.5` the value is `+15381.648528026303809`; the
  commented-out test in the report simply had the sign wrong (Wolfram,
  mpmath and the recurrence agree).
* "not recommended for even n with large |x|".  This is real, but it is
  a loss of digits to cancellation, not a wrong result: for even `n` the
  correction sum is much larger than the value, and the accuracy falls
  off as `n` and `|x|` grow.  The tested vectors use tolerances from
  `TEST_TOL1` to `TEST_TOL5` accordingly, and the reported error bar
  grows with the cancellation.

The report's headline argument is `-4503599224717311/536870912`, i.e.
`-8388607.25 + 2^-29`; the expected `124.02510962421644344751` is
correct for that argument (it is `4 pi^3` less a small correction from
the `2^-29` offset).  Using `x = -8388607.25` exactly gives
`124.02510672119928` instead.

Verification: values against mpmath at 40 digits (`polygamma`).  The
sum for the headline case has 8.4 million terms, which is why the
summation is compensated.  `specfunc/test_sf.c` gains 17 value vectors
(`n = 2..5`, arguments straddling the poles at -1 and -2, `|x|` up to
10.5, and the report's argument) plus five checks that the poles and
negative orders remain `GSL_EDOM`.  Negative control: with the change
reverted all 17 value vectors fail with `GSL_EDOM`.  Full `ctest` suite
56/56 on MSVC after the change.

The documented domain becomes `n >= 0, x != 0, -1, -2, ...` in both
`doc/specfunc-psi.rst` and `doc_texinfo/specfunc-psi.texi`, with a note
on the loss of digits for even `n` and large `|x|`.

Files: `specfunc/psi.c`, `specfunc/test_sf.c`, `doc/specfunc-psi.rst`,
`doc_texinfo/specfunc-psi.texi`.


## [upstream] specfunc: spherical Bessel Y functions use the system sine/cosine

Commit: a08ef2f7f

`gsl_sf_bessel_y0_e`, `y1_e` and `y2_e` evaluated their large-x
oscillatory branch through `gsl_sf_cos_e()` / `gsl_sf_sin_e()`.  That
argument reduction represents pi/4 with three doubles and loses the
reduced angle once the quotient reaches ~1e18, so it returns values
outside [-1,1] (for example `gsl_sf_cos_e(1e20) = -2.86e42`).  The Y
functions therefore did not decay like 1/x but grew with the garbage,
reporting `GSL_SUCCESS` throughout: `y0(1e20)` was `+2.86e22` instead of
`-7.64e-21`.

The branch now calls the system `cos()` and `sin()` directly, exactly as
`gsl_sf_bessel_j0_e`, `j1_e` and `j2_e` do since commits cd2dd0519 and
bd5b94b47 for the same defect, and reports a relative error of a few
machine epsilons instead of the 100 % error the trig functions returned.
The small-x series branches are untouched; `gsl_sf_bessel_yl_e` and
`yl_array` inherit the fix through their `y0`/`y1` seeds.

Seven vectors for `y0_e`, `y1_e` and `y2_e` at 1e18, 1e20 and 1e22 come
from `sqrt(pi/(2x)) Y_{l+1/2}(x)` evaluated with mpmath at 40 digits.
Negative control: with `bessel_y.c` reverted all seven fail, e.g.
`y0(1e18)` returns `+6.8613183520047959e-17 +/- 6.86e-17` against
`-1.18371990218710732612e-19`; with the fix `specfunc_test` reports no
failure.  No documentation change: the values were always meant to be
the asymptotics.

Files: `specfunc/bessel_y.c`, `specfunc/test_bessel.c`.

