## [upstream] specfunc: exact argument reduction for sine and cosine

Commit: 8a46ec7cf

`gsl_sf_sin_e` and `gsl_sf_cos_e` reduced the argument with a three-term
representation of pi/4.  Its error in the reduced angle grows like
`eps*|x|`, so above `|x| ~ 1e9` the accuracy degrades and beyond
`|x| ~ 1e16` the result is meaningless while the status remains
`GSL_SUCCESS`: `gsl_sf_cos_e(1e20)` returned `-2.86e42`.  The same
reduction is reached through `gsl_sf_sin_err_e`, `gsl_sf_cos_err_e` and
`gsl_sf_sinc_e`.

The fdlibm Payne-Hanek reduction (`__rem_pio2_large`, tables included,
with the Sun notice) is added and used above `|x| = 2^21`, where the fast
path is still exact.  The reduced argument is carried as a double-double
`y0 + y1` and folded into the existing Chebyshev series to first order, so
the reduction is exact over the whole double range and the result is good
to a few ulp for every finite argument.

Verification: two million deterministic random doubles across the full
exponent range, compared against the platform libm (independently checked
against mpmath), disagree nowhere by more than `1e-13`, with a worst
absolute error of `2.2e-16`.  Eight vectors at 1.1e12, 1e18, 1e20 and
1e22 (mpmath, 40 digits) fail with `trig.c` reverted, e.g. `sin(1e18)`
gives `-6.15e1` against `-9.93e-1`.  With the change the full `ctest`
suite is 56/56.

`gsl_sf_angle_restrict_*` keeps its own reduction and its documented
`GSL_ELOSS` above `0.0625/eps`; that contract is not part of this change.

Files: `specfunc/trig.c`, `specfunc/test_sf.c`,
`doc/specfunc-trig.rst`, `doc_texinfo/specfunc-trig.texi`.

