## [upstream] specfunc: avoid integer overflow in the Bessel Jn/Yn branch tests

Commit: a43fc0055

`gsl_sf_bessel_Jn_e` and `gsl_sf_bessel_Yn_e` chose their asymptotic
branch with `(n*n + 1.0)`, where `n` is an `int`.  For `n > 46340` the
product overflows - signed overflow is undefined, and the wrapped
negative value made the comparison `GSL_ROOT4_DBL_EPSILON*x > (n*n+1.0)`
true - so both functions took the large-argument asymptotic expansion
outside its range.  `Jn` returned a NaN while reporting `GSL_SUCCESS`
(`gsl_sf_bessel_Jn_e(46341, 51471.45)` was `nan`, Savannah bug #40755);
`Yn` returned a finite but wrong value (`Yn(46341, 51471.45)` was
`-0.0012152637` against the true `+0.0044148266`).

The product is now computed in double.  `Jn` and `Yn` route to the
Olver asymptotic (`n > 50`) and match `scipy.special.jv`/`yv` to the
last digits.  Ten vectors at `n = 46341..46345`,
`x = 51471.451912555509` are added; the references are scipy's `jv`
and `yv` (an independent Amos implementation).

Negative control: with the source change reverted, `specfunc_test`
reports NaN for all five `Jn` vectors and a wrong value for all five
`Yn` vectors.  Full `ctest` suite 56/56 on MSVC.

Files: `specfunc/bessel_Jn.c`, `specfunc/bessel_Yn.c`,
`specfunc/test_bessel.c`.

Savannah bug #40755.

The same `n*n` also appears in `bessel_In.c` and `bessel_Kn.c`.  It was
left alone: for `In` the overflow does not change the selected branch,
and for `Kn` it does, but both `Knu_scaled_asympx` and
`Knu_scaled_asymp_unif` are wildly inaccurate for `n` near `x`
(scipy's exponentially scaled `kve` overflows there), so the fix would
swap one wrong approximation for another rather than correct anything.


## [upstream] specfunc: use a non-vanishing endpoint in the half-integer Jnu

Commit: e6e34279a

`gsl_sf_bessel_Jnupos_e`, in the `x >= 2` branch, obtains `J_nu` by
normalizing the backward recurrence at order `mu = nu - N` through the
unnormalized `J_mu`.  For half-integer `nu`, `N = nu + 1/2` gives
`mu = -1/2`, and `J_{-1/2}(x) = sqrt(2/(pi x)) cos(x)` vanishes at
`x = (k + 1/2) pi`.  At `x = 3 pi / 2` the single recurrence step
cancels to exactly zero - the CF1 ratio `J_{1/2}/J_{-1/2}` equals the
coefficient `2 nu / x` - and the normalization divides by it, so
`gsl_sf_bessel_Jnu_e(0.5, 1.5*M_PI)` returned a NaN with `GSL_SUCCESS`
(Savannah bug #42042).  A step of `1e-15` in `x` already gave the
correct `-0.36755259694786135`.

Both endpoints `mu = -1/2` and `mu = +1/2` are admissible for
half-integer `nu`.  When `mu = -1/2` and `|cos(x)| < |sin(x)|` the code
now shifts to `mu = +1/2` (and `N` down by one), using the endpoint
with the larger `|J_mu|`.  Half-integer `nu` reaches this branch only
for `x >= sqrt(10 (nu+1)) > 2`, so the `x < 2` path is untouched.

New vector `J_{1/2}(3 pi / 2) = -0.36755259694786135` (mpmath, 40
digits).  Negative control: with the change reverted the vector reports
NaN (`specfunc_test` FAILs on `gsl_sf_bessel_Jnu_e(0.5, 1.5*M_PI)`).
Full `ctest` suite 56/56 on MSVC.

Files: `specfunc/bessel_Jnu.c`, `specfunc/test_bessel.c`.

