## [upstream] sys: check for inf/nan in gsl_hypot3

Commit: c1df353ae

`gsl_hypot3(x, y, z)` forms `w = max(|x|, |y|, |z|)` and divides the
magnitudes by it.  With an infinite argument the ratio `inf/inf`
evaluates to NaN, so `gsl_hypot3(inf, 1, 1)` and
`gsl_hypot3(inf, NaN, NaN)` both returned NaN instead of the `+Inf`
required by the C99 specification for `hypot`.  The reporter (the GSL
maintainer) suggested implementing the function as
`gsl_hypot(gsl_hypot(x, y), z)`, which would inherit the exception
handling already in `gsl_hypot`; that cannot be taken as written because
`gsl_hypot` reported an overflow for infinite arguments at the time
(the subject of the companion fix below), so the check is carried out
explicitly here instead.

`gsl_hypot3` now returns `+Inf` as soon as any argument is `+-Inf`,
even when another is NaN, matching `gsl_hypot`; a NaN with no infinite
argument still propagates.  The magnitudes `xabs`, `yabs`, `zabs` are
hoisted above the guard so the guard runs first.

Tests: six vectors are added to `sys/test.c` for the three infinite
positions, for infinity together with NaN, and for NaN with finite
arguments.  Negative control: with the guard reverted `sys_test` reports
five failures, all in the new vectors (the plain-NaN vectors passed by
accident before, since `max` falls through to a finite argument and
`NaN/finite` is NaN).  With the guard applied `sys_test` is 341/341 and
`ctest` is 56/56 on MSVC.

Documentation: the infinity rule is added to `gsl_hypot` and
`gsl_hypot3` in `doc/math.rst` and `doc_texinfo/math.texi`.

