## [rejected] specfunc: port the Wigner d-matrix (gsl_sf_wigner_drot)

Deferred, not applied.  No patch.

Savannah bug #46677 asks why `contrib/wigner.h` was never ported into
`specfunc/`.  Most of it was: the 3j, 6j and 9j symbols now live in
`specfunc/coupling.c` and are declared in `gsl/gsl_sf_coupling.h`.  The
one function left behind is `gsl_sf_wigner_drot` / `_e`, the Wigner
(small) d-matrix, whose only home is `contrib/wigner.c`.

That is a feature request - a new public function, its header entry, its
documentation and its tests - rather than a bug fix, so it falls outside
the eligibility rule for this review and is deferred rather than taken or
rejected on the merits.  The submitter's own probe (copy the file, rename
to GSL conventions, add the source to the build) is the shape of the
change; it would also need test vectors and a decision on the two-j /
two-m integer parameter convention and the `theta` argument.

Two things to keep in mind if it is ever picked up:

* Adding the file means touching both build systems: `specfunc/Makefile.am`
  and the generated `cmake/gsl_sources.cmake` (regenerate with
  `python cmake/regen_sources.py`), as `AGENTS.md` requires.

* `contrib/wigner.c` is *not* a more accurate implementation than
  `specfunc`.  Its 3j/6j/9j go through `lndelta_e` (log factorials), so
  they do not overflow the way the factorial normalisation in
  `specfunc/coupling.c` can, but they are still direct alternating sums,
  so they lose accuracy for large `j` in exactly the way Savannah bug
  `#68312` reports.  Porting the file would therefore not close `#68312`,
  which needs a stable algorithm.  That algorithm is now in place
  separately: `gsl_sf_coupling_6j_e()` uses the Schulten-Gordon
