## [upstream] specfunc: reject a NaN argument in the complete elliptic integrals

Commit: 14d595eb8

`gsl_sf_ellint_Kcomp_e(GSL_NAN)` and `_Ecomp_e(GSL_NAN)` were reported
(Savannah bug #31362, 2010, Will M. Farr) to loop forever, because the
Carlson recurrences RC/RD/RF/RJ had no iteration limit and the convergence
test `epslon < errtol` is never satisfied for a NaN.  Upstream commit
`9a7cf12e0` added the `nmax` counters, so the loop now stops and reports
`GSL_EMAXITER`; the same symptom was re-reported as Savannah bug #48702
(2016) as an OS-dependent "stall" - on some systems the loop ran the full
10000 iterations, on others the floating-point behaviour differed.  What
was left was the `FIXME` in `specfunc/test_sf.c`: "we are accepting
MAXITER as the return code, but really this should be changed to EINVAL".

A NaN argument is not a convergence failure, so it is now rejected before
any iteration starts.  `gsl_sf_ellint_Kcomp_e`, `_Ecomp_e`, `_Dcomp_e` and
`_Pcomp_e` test `gsl_isnan(k)` (and `gsl_isnan(n)` for `Pcomp`) as their
first statement and return `GSL_EDOM` with NaN through `DOMAIN_ERROR`.
The test is deliberately `isnan` and not `isfinite`: `k = +-inf` already
satisfies the existing `k*k >= 1.0` domain test and must keep returning
`GSL_EDOM`, and `k*k` for a NaN is NaN so the old comparison silently fell
through.  This is the same treatment the rest of `specfunc` gives a
non-finite argument.

This reverses a policy recorded earlier in this file (the `sin_pi`/`cos_pi`
entry, Savannah bug #57978), where NaN was deliberately left to propagate
and surface as `GSL_EMAXITER`.  That reasoning was written for functions
with no natural domain bound; a *bounded-domain* function such as these -
defined only for `|k| < 1` - should reject a NaN as out of domain, and the
form `k*k >= 1.0` that made `inf` a domain error but let NaN through was an
accident of the comparison, not a decision.

The vectors in `specfunc/test_sf.c` for bug #31362 now assert `GSL_EDOM`
for `Kcomp`, `Ecomp`, `Dcomp` and `Pcomp`.  With the guard removed they
assert `GSL_EMAXITER` (and, before upstream `9a7cf12e0`, the call did not
return at all).  The `#48702` report is the same defect by its second
symptom and is closed with it.

Files: `specfunc/ellint.c`, `specfunc/test_sf.c`,
`doc/specfunc-ellint.rst`, `doc_texinfo/specfunc-ellint.texi`.

Savannah bugs #31362 and #48702.


## [upstream] specfunc: terminate the incomplete gamma function for large negative a

Commit: 932087bcd

`gsl_sf_gamma_inc_e(a, x)` builds `Gamma(a,x)` for negative `a` by
starting from the half-integer value `Gamma(da,x)` and stepping the order
down one unit at a time:

    do {
      shift = exp(-x + (alpha-1)*log(x));
      gax   = (gax - shift) / (alpha - 1);
      alpha -= 1;
    } while (alpha > a);

For `a < -2^53` the loop reaches an `alpha` of magnitude at least `2^53`,
where the spacing of doubles has grown to 2 and `alpha - 1.0` rounds back
to `alpha`.  The decrement then stops advancing, `alpha > a` is never
satisfied, and the call hangs (Savannah bug #50459, Fonenantsoa Maurica).
Reproduced here: `gsl_sf_gamma_inc_e(-1.0e25, 0.1)` was still spinning
after eight seconds.

For `a < -2^53` with `x` small enough to reach this branch (the caller
only arrives for `x <= 0.25`), `x` is negligible against `|a|` and
`Gamma(a,x)` is far below the smallest normal number - `|x|^(|a|)` is
`1e-250` for the reported case - so the function now returns zero directly
with `GSL_EUNDRFLW`, before the recurrence begins.  The threshold is the
exact double `9007199254740992.0` (`2^53`); at or below it the decrement
still changes `alpha`, so the existing branch is left untouched.

An equivalent fix would be to break the loop when `alpha - 1 == alpha`,
but the underflow is genuine in this range and returning zero is the
correct answer, so the guard is simpler and does not rely on the loop
detecting its own lack of progress.

Verified: the reported call now returns `val = 0`, `GSL_EUNDRFLW` in
microseconds.  The new vector in `specfunc/test_gamma.c` fails (hangs)
with the guard removed.  Full suite 56/56.

Files: `specfunc/gamma_inc.c`, `specfunc/test_gamma.c`.

Savannah bug #50459.


## [upstream] specfunc: exact P + Q = 1 in the incomplete gamma series region

Commit: 6f23a4cb5

The regularized incomplete gamma functions satisfy `P(a,x) + Q(a,x) = 1`,
but the two were computed independently and on different branch conditions
(Brian Gough, Savannah bug #21836): for `(a, x) = (0.3, 1.0)` the sum was
`1 - 1.44e-15` rather than exactly 1, and over a coarse grid the residual
reached `1.9e-12`.

`gsl_sf_gamma_inc_Q_e` now returns the exact complement `1 - P` in the
region where `gsl_sf_gamma_inc_P_e` evaluates its series *and* forming the
complement costs no accuracy:

    a >= 0.2  and  x < 20  and  x <= a + 2 sqrt(a)

There `P` is at most about `1 - 1e-3`, so `1 - P` reproduces `Q` to full
precision (checked against `Q`'s own continued fraction: the relative
difference stays below `1e-13`), and `P + Q == 1` bit for bit.  Outside
that window the subtraction would lose digits - at `x = 10`, `a = 1`,
`1 - P` is wrong in the 10th significant figure - so `Q`'s own continued
fraction is kept, and for `a < 0.2` the dedicated small-`a` cancellation
series in `gamma_inc_Q_series` is kept, because it exists precisely to
avoid that cancellation there.

The reported case `(0.3, 1.0)` is in the window and now gives
`P + Q == 1` exactly; `Q(1, 10)` and `Q(0.001, 0.001)` are outside it and
return their previous values unchanged.  A grid assertion in
`specfunc/test_gamma.c` covers the window and counts 0 failures; with the
new branch removed it reports 1099 failures.  Full suite 56/56.

Files: `specfunc/gamma_inc.c`, `specfunc/test_gamma.c`.

Savannah bug #21836.


## [rejected] specfunc: "incomplete gamma functions flipped"

Savannah bug #45925 reported that `gsl_sf_gamma_inc(1, 3)` returns
`0.0497871` while `gsl_sf_gamma_inc_P(1, 3)` returns `0.950213`, and asked
whether the two are "flipped".  They are not.  `gsl_sf_gamma_inc` is the
*unnormalized* upper incomplete gamma `Gamma(a,x)`, which for `a > 0`
equals `Gamma(a) Q(a,x) = Gamma(a)(1 - P(a,x))`; the complement of `P` is
`Q`, not `Gamma(a,x)`.  The reported numbers confirm it:
`Gamma(1,3) = Q(1,3) = e^-3 = 0.049787...` and `P(1,3) = 1 - e^-3 =
0.950212...`, which is exactly what the library returns.  The tracker
comment by Bill Maier reached the same conclusion.

The report also asserted "you have such a bug in the beta as well",
comparing `beta_inc(a,b,x)` with `beta_inc(b,a,1-x)`.  That comparison has
the same shape: `gsl_sf_beta_inc` is the regularized incomplete beta
`I_x(a,b)`, whose complement `1 - I_x(a,b)` equals `I_{1-x}(b,a)` as a
mathematical identity but is not reproduced bit for bit by two separate
continued-fraction evaluations, exactly as for `P`/`Q` above.  No defect.

Rather than change correct code, the naming is clarified: the
`gsl_sf_gamma_inc` entry of both manuals now states the relation to
`Q(a,x)` and `P(a,x)` explicitly, with the `Gamma(1,3) = e^-3` example,
so the confusion does not recur.  Files: `doc/specfunc-gamma.rst`,
`doc_texinfo/specfunc-gamma.texi`.

Savannah bug #45925.


## [upstream] specfunc: evaluate the terminating 2F1 for any non-positive integer

Commit: 2aa89bae8

`gsl_sf_hyperg_2F1_e()` reaches the terminating Gauss series only through
the `fabs(a) < 10 && fabs(b) < 10` branch, and the series itself sits behind
the `|x| < 1` convergence check.  When `a` or `b` is zero or a negative
integer the Gauss series terminates and `2F1` is a *polynomial*, finite for
every `x`; the value is well defined even where the infinite series does
not converge.  Those calls were nevertheless rejected:

    2F1(-1,-13,1,0.651439)   GSL_EUNIMPL      (Savannah #50711)
    2F1(-10,2,0.5,0.5)       GSL_EUNIMPL      (Savannah #53905)
    2F1(-1,-10,1,0.5)        GSL_EUNIMPL      (Savannah #39056, Wolpert)
    2F1(-1,-1,-0.5,1.5)      GSL_EDOM         (Savannah #21835)
    2F1(0,1,1,11)            GSL_EDOM         (Savannah #21835)

The first two fail because `-13` and `-10` are outside the hard-coded
magnitude gate; the `x >= 1` cases fail at the domain check.  The Wolpert
and Hatef Monajemi vectors of #39056 were commented out of the tests when
#39056 was opened; the Monajemi case was fixed with the integer-`d`
reflection work (`e4c4ac326`), the Wolpert case is fixed here.

The terminating case is now dispatched to `hyperg_2F1_series()` before the
`|x| < 1` domain check and independently of the `|a|,|b| < 10` gate.  The
negative-integer-`c` cancellation test is preserved, so the domain errors
for an uncancelled pole in `c` are unchanged.

References were computed independently, with mpmath at 50 digits
(`hyp2f1`) or from elementary closed forms:

    2F1(-1,-13,1,0.651439) = 9.4687069999999998737
    2F1(-10,2,0.5,0.5)     = 0.077832384333932321548
    2F1(-1,-1,-0.5,1.5)    = -2 exactly
    2F1(-1,-10,1,0.5)      = 6 exactly

Negative control: with the dispatch reverted, `specfunc_test` reports 11
failures - the ten new vectors plus the summary line.  The `ldnlwm` vector
that shared the `#if 0` block with the Wolpert one passes either way; it was
enabled as well.  With the change the `specfunc` suite and the full CTest
suite pass.

