## [upstream] interpolation: average the Akima slopes when a weight vanishes

Commit: 0efb5f87f

Savannah bug #52351 ("akima.c array indexing") reports a misbehaving
Akima spline.  There is no out-of-bounds access: the defect is in
`akima_calc()`, in the handling of the weight denominator

    NE = |m[i+1] - m[i]| + |m[i-1] - m[i-2]|

and its counterpart for the tangent at the right end of the interval.
When the two differences are equal the denominator is zero and GSL
substituted one of the adjacent slopes: `m[i]` for the tangent at
`x_i`, and `m[i]` again for the tangent at `x_{i+1}`.  The resulting
spline is not C1: on the demo2 data (`x = 0..7`,
`y = 0,0,2,0,-1,-2,-1,0`) it has a slope of -1 then +1 at the minimum
at `x = 5`.

The indeterminate limit is the average of the two adjacent slopes.
The code now uses `0.5*(m[i-1] + m[i])` for the tangent at `x_i` and
`0.5*(m[i] + m[i+1])` for the tangent at `x_{i+1}`, and always forms
the cubic coefficients so the averaged tangent is used.  This is not
the reporter's proposed `m[i+1]`, which removes the corner but
overshoots the minimum and still differs from the reference by 0.14.

Verified against the independent scipy implementation
`Akima1DInterpolator(method="akima")`: the fixed code agrees to
4.4e-16 on the demo2 data, the old code was off by 0.140625, and the
reporter's variant likewise.  A vector at `x = 4.25 ... 5.75` was
added to `interpolation/test.c`; with the source change reverted all
six points fail (e.g. -1.5 observed vs -1.625 expected at `x = 4.5`).

Files: `interpolation/akima.c`, `interpolation/test.c`.

Savannah bug #52351.


## [upstream] eigen: rescale and bound the symmetric QR iteration

Commit: 7d586d89b

Savannah bug #31426 reports an infinite loop in `gsl_eigen_symm()` on
a 1024x1024 matrix (the attachment is no longer reachable).  The loop
is real and reproducible with a much simpler input: a symmetric
tridiagonal matrix whose entries are subnormal.  The chopping
threshold `GSL_DBL_EPSILON * (|d_i| + |d_{i+1}|)` underflows to zero,
so `chop_small_elements()` can never remove the subdiagonal; the QR
sweep then reaches an exact fixed point (for `N = 16` with all entries
`GSL_DBL_MIN`, `d` and `sd` are unchanged after the first sweep) and
`b` never decrements.  This is the same class of failure as #28096,
which the 2009 `qrstep` guard did not cover.

`gsl_eigen_symm()` and `gsl_eigen_symmv()` now rescale the referenced
(diagonal and lower) triangle by a power of two so that its largest
entry lands in [0.5,1), run the existing decomposition and QR sweep,
and scale the eigenvalues back with `ldexp()`.  Power-of-two scaling
is exact and the Householder and QR steps are scale-equivariant, so
results for normal matrices are bit-identical.  As a safety net the
sweep count per unreduced block is bounded and `GSL_EMAXITER` is
returned if it is exceeded, so the function can no longer hang.

Verified with a faithful transcription of the loop and against
`numpy.linalg.eigvalsh` and the closed form `1 + 2 cos(k pi/(N+1))`;
a 16x16 `GSL_DBL_MIN` tridiagonal matrix was added to `eigen/test.c`.
With the two source changes reverted `eigen_test` hangs (observed with
a 12 s timeout); with them it returns the correct eigenvalues through
both entry points.  The new `GSL_EMAXITER` return is documented in
both manuals.

Note: the original `mat.bin` attachment is unavailable, so this
addresses a reproduced class of non-termination rather than that exact
matrix.

Files: `eigen/symm.c`, `eigen/symmv.c`, `eigen/test.c`,
`doc/eigen.rst`, `doc_texinfo/eigen.texi`.

Savannah bug #31426.


