## [rejected] integration: qagiu on cosh(x) exp(-cosh(x))

Not a library bug; no change.  Savannah bug #59911 asks why
`gsl_integration_qagiu()` fails on

    int_0^inf cosh(x) exp(-cosh(x)) dx

with "bad integrand behavior".  The `qagiu` transformation evaluates
the integrand at `x = (1 - t)/t`, which grows without bound as
`t -> 0`.  For `x > ~710.5`, `cosh(x)` overflows to `+Inf`,
`exp(-cosh(x))` underflows to 0, and the product is `NaN`; that NaN
reaches the quadrature.  With a 1000-interval workspace the call
returns `GSL_EMAXITER` with a NaN result.  GSL cannot rescue an
integrand that is not finite at the points the transformation probes.
The Savannah thread's own answer - substitute `u = sinh(x)`, giving
`int_0^inf exp(-sqrt(1+u^2)) du` - is the correct advice.

Savannah bug #59911.


## [upstream] histogram: exact uniform bin limits for integer ranges

Commit: f9c977f52

`make_uniform()` built each limit as
`((n-i)/n)*xmin + (i/n)*xmax`, rounding both coefficients before
applying them.  With an integer span equal to the number of bins -
`xmin = 0`, `xmax = 60`, `n = 60` - the boundary `range[31]` came out
as `31.000000000000004`, so the integer value 31 fell in bin 30, and
in general an integer boundary could drift by an ulp.

The limit is now evaluated as `xmin + (xmax - xmin) * i / n`.  Taking
the product with the span before dividing keeps the intermediate exact
whenever the span and `n` are whole numbers: a 60-bin histogram over
`[0,60]` has `range[31] = 31` exactly.  The same helper is duplicated
in `init2d.c` for the two-dimensional histograms and is changed there
too.

Three formulas were compared against exact rational arithmetic: the
reported midpoint `0.5*(xmin + i*dx) + 0.5*(xmax - (n-i)*dx)`, the
single `xmin + i*dx`, and the chosen `xmin + (xmax - xmin)*i/n`.  Only
the last is exact both for the reported case and for the correctly
rounded boundaries of a 10-bin histogram over `[0,1]`, where the other
two move `0.1`, `0.2`, `0.4` or `0.6` by an ulp.  The division can
still move the end limits, so `range[0]` and `range[n]` are pinned to
`xmin` and `xmax`.

Test vectors in `histogram/test1d.c` and `test2d.c` check the exact
interior limit, that an integer lands in its own bin, and that
`range[0]` is preserved for `n = 107`.  With the old formula the new
assertions report 5 failures (3 one-dimensional, 2 two-dimensional);
the suite is 56/56 on MSVC.

Files: `histogram/init.c`, `histogram/init2d.c`, `histogram/test1d.c`,
`histogram/test2d.c`.

See Savannah bug #38548.


